The signal-to-background ratio obtained with a particular isolation cut is given by

\[SB(r_{iso})=\frac{N_{sig}\cdot R_{sig}(r_{iso})}{N_{bkg}\cdot R_{bkg}(r_{iso})}= SB(0)\cdot\frac{R_{sig}(r_{iso})}{R_{bkg}(r_{iso})} \tag{5.5}\]

where \(SB(0)\) is the ratio of prompt photon events to background events without isolation cuts, i.e. as generated. \(\frac{R_{sig}(r_{iso})}{R_{bkg}(r_{iso})}\) represents the discriminating power of the isolation cut \(r_{iso}\) and is calculated by taking the ratio of the signal and background efficiencies shown in Figure 5.25. The discriminating power against all types of background is shown in the left panel of Figure 5.26. In particular the discriminating power against the photon background from \(\pi^{0}\) decays is shown in the right panel of Figure 5.26.

